\section{Representing time}

We work with integer minutes since midnight internally.  Integers make
ordering, overlap and adjacency tests plain comparisons, whereas
comparing [[HH:MM]] strings would only work by accident of zero padding.
The working day is a pair of constants so that no magic numbers appear
in the algorithm.

<<constants>>=
DAY_START = 8 * 60
DAY_END = 17 * 60
@

\section{Parsing and formatting}

A booking line must have the exact shape [[HH:MM-HH:MM]].  We validate
with a regular expression rather than splitting on characters, so that
malformed input is rejected with a clear message instead of failing
later with an obscure [[IndexError]].  We also reject bookings whose end
is not after their start, since they cannot denote a time span.

<<imports>>=
import re
import sys
<<constants>>=
SLOT_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d{1,2}):(\d{2})-(\d{1,2}):(\d{2})$")
<<functions>>=
def parse_slot(line):
    """Parse `HH:MM-HH:MM` into a (start, end) pair of minutes.

    Raise `ValueError` if the text is malformed or the end is not
    after the start.
    """
    match = SLOT_RE.match(line.strip())
    if match is None:
        raise ValueError(f"bad time slot: {line.strip()!r}")
    h1, m1, h2, m2 = (int(g) for g in match.groups())
    if m1 > 59 or m2 > 59 or h1 > 23 or h2 > 24:
        raise ValueError(f"time out of range: {line.strip()!r}")
    start, end = h1 * 60 + m1, h2 * 60 + m2
    if end <= start:
        raise ValueError(f"slot must end after it starts: {line.strip()!r}")
    return start, end
@

Formatting is the inverse, always zero padded so the output has the same
shape as the input.

<<functions>>=
def format_slot(start, end):
    """Format a (start, end) pair of minutes as `HH:MM-HH:MM`."""
    s_h, s_m = divmod(start, 60)
    e_h, e_m = divmod(end, 60)
    return f"{s_h:02d}:{s_m:02d}-{e_h:02d}:{e_m:02d}"
@

Let's verify that parsing and formatting are inverse, and that bad input
is rejected:

<<test functions>>=
import pytest


def test_parse_and_format_roundtrip():
  assert parse_slot("09:00-10:30") == (540, 630)
  assert format_slot(540, 630) == "09:00-10:30"


@pytest.mark.parametrize(
  "bad",
  ["", "9-10", "10:00-09:00", "10:00-10:00", "25:00-26:00", "09:60-10:00"],
)
def test_parse_rejects_bad_input(bad):
  with pytest.raises(ValueError):
    parse_slot(bad)
@

\section{Merging bookings}

Bookings arrive in any order and may overlap or touch.  If we sort them
by start time, then any booking that begins no later than the end of
the current merged block belongs to that block: everything earlier has
already been absorbed, so a later start can only be inside or just after
the block.  Using [[<=]] rather than [[<]] fuses touching bookings,
which is harmless since a zero-length gap is not a free slot anyway, and
it keeps the merged list strictly separated.

<<functions>>=
def merge(slots):
    """Return the sorted, disjoint union of (start, end) pairs."""
    merged = []
    for start, end in sorted(slots):
        if merged and start <= merged[-1][1]:
            merged[-1] = (merged[-1][0], max(merged[-1][1], end))
        else:
            merged.append((start, end))
    return merged
@

We check unordered, overlapping, touching and nested bookings:

<<test functions>>=
def test_merge_overlap_touch_nested_and_unordered():
  slots = [(600, 660), (540, 600), (630, 700), (650, 660), (800, 810)]
  assert merge(slots) == [(540, 700), (800, 810)]


def test_merge_empty():
  assert merge([]) == []
@

\section{Finding the free slots}

The free time is the complement of the merged bookings within the
working day.  We keep a cursor that starts at the beginning of the day.
Because the merged bookings are sorted and disjoint, the space between
the cursor and the next booking is free, and afterwards the cursor
jumps to that booking's end.  Bookings reaching outside the working day
are clipped by [[max]] and [[min]] so that they never produce slots
outside 08:00--17:00; a booking wholly outside leaves the cursor
untouched.

<<functions>>=
def free_slots(bookings, day_start=DAY_START, day_end=DAY_END):
    """Return the free (start, end) pairs between `day_start` and `day_end`."""
    free = []
    cursor = day_start
    for start, end in merge(bookings):
        if start > cursor:
            free.append((cursor, min(start, day_end)))
        cursor = max(cursor, end)
        if cursor >= day_end:
            break
    if cursor < day_end:
        free.append((cursor, day_end))
    return free
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_no_bookings_whole_day_free():
  assert free_slots([]) == [(DAY_START, DAY_END)]


def test_gaps_between_and_around_bookings():
  booked = [(540, 630), (630, 720), (660, 690)]
  assert free_slots(booked) == [(480, 540), (720, 1020)]


def test_bookings_outside_day_are_clipped():
  assert free_slots([(0, 9 * 60), (16 * 60, 20 * 60)]) == [(540, 960)]
  assert free_slots([(0, 7 * 60)]) == [(DAY_START, DAY_END)]
  assert free_slots([(18 * 60, 19 * 60)]) == [(DAY_START, DAY_END)]


def test_fully_booked_day():
  assert free_slots([(480, 700), (700, 1020)]) == []
@

\section{The command line}

The program reads standard input, ignoring blank lines so that a
trailing newline or separating blank is not an error.  The core is a
function from lines to lines, which keeps the I/O shell trivial and the
logic testable without touching [[sys.stdin]].

<<functions>>=
def free_slot_lines(lines):
    """Map booking lines to the formatted free-slot lines."""
    bookings = [parse_slot(line) for line in lines if line.strip()]
    return [format_slot(s, e) for s, e in free_slots(bookings)]


def main():
    """Read bookings from standard input and print the free slots."""
    try:
        for line in free_slot_lines(sys.stdin):
            print(line)
    except ValueError as err:
        sys.exit(f"freeslots: {err}")
@

Finally an end-to-end check of the text interface:

<<test functions>>=
def test_free_slot_lines_end_to_end():
  lines = ["13:00-14:00\n", "\n", "09:00-10:30\n", "10:00-11:00\n"]
  assert free_slot_lines(lines) == [
    "08:00-09:00",
    "11:00-13:00",
    "14:00-17:00",
  ]
